# Tools installed somewhere other than the prefix, on purpose.
#
# tool <TAB> where it is now <TAB> why
#
# The prefix being empty for one of these is the intended state, so it is
# reported as `elsewhere` rather than `uninstalled`, and `install` leaves it
# alone. Installing it anyway would put a second copy on PATH ahead of the real
# one and recreate the shadowing this exists to record.
#
# The path is CHECKED rather than believed. If nothing is there the state is
# `elsewhere-gone`, because a declaration nothing verifies is a way to silence
# a tool that really has disappeared.
#
# A copy turning up in the prefix anyway is still reported normally. Two
# installs racing each other on PATH is the thing this tool exists to notice.
#
# Like local.tsv, keep this in ~/bin and reach it through a symlink at
# ~/.config/tooling-sync/elsewhere.tsv: ~/.config has no git history and is in
# no backup set, so a declaration kept only there has no record of when it was
# made or why.
#
# ordane	/usr/bin/ordane	installed from its own .deb; ~/bin would shadow it
